sanity-check collisions\nmulti = {k:v for k,v in bykey.items() if len(set(s for s,_ in v))>1}\nprint(f\"total distinct keys: {len(bykey)}, keys in multiple datasets: {len(multi)}\")\n# Print names for interesting keys\nfor k in sorted(multi):\n    if any(t in k for t in ['atletico','athletico','america','sport','santos','flamengo','palmeiras','corinthians','gremio','sao paulo','bahia','vitoria','guarani','paysandu','remo','ceara','fortaleza','goias','criciuma','parana','nautico','abc','inter','nacional','juventude','portuguesa','ponte','figueirense','chapecoense','avai','cuiaba']):\n        print(k, '=>', sorted(multi[k]))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788226581885,"end":1788226581889}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good to know:\n- Libertadores: some matches have '-' goals (abandoned/unplayed, e.g. Boca Juniors vs River Plate 2015 famously abandoned). Should handle gracefully (match without result or skip in stats).\n- Cup rounds: numeric 1-8 only; \"finals\" aren't explicitly marked. Round 8 has 14 matches? That's odd — probably round 8 = semifinal-ish? Actually Copa do Brasil has up to round 7 or 8 in some years (final = two legs). With rounds numeric, \"final\" identification is tricky. I could identify finals as the last round of a season (max round per season). Round 8 (14 matches) is strange though — maybe multiple matches per round in different seasons. Let me not overthink; for \"finals\" I'll offer the latest stage/round matches per season.\n\n- Brasileirao_Matches covers 2012-2022 (4180 = 11 × 380).\n- novo covers 2003-2019 (6886... not exactly 17×380=6460; 2003-2005 had different team counts: 2003 had 46 games? Actually 2003-2005 had 46 teams/rounds? The counts don't matter).\n- BR-Football covers 2014-2023, tournaments: Serie A, Serie B, Serie C, Copa do Brasil.\n- Libertadores 2013-2022.\n- Cup 2012-2021.\n- FIFA: 827 Brazilians; 60 players with empty position.\n\nNow the design. I'll build a Python package `brasil_mcp` in the current directory with:\n\n```\nsrc/brasil_mcp/\n  __init__.py\n  models.py       # dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamRecord, StandingEntry, HeadToHead, etc.\n  normalize.py    # accent stripping, team name canonicalization, alias table\n  dates.py        # multi-format date parsing\n  loaders.py      # per-file loaders -> list[Match]/list[Player]\n  store.py        # SoccerDatastore: loads all, indexes (team -> matches), provides query methods\n  queries.py      # higher-level query functions returning formatted results (dicts)\n  formatting.py?  # maybe merge into queries\n  server.py       # MCPServer (mcp v2) exposing tools\n  cli.py          # argparse CLI for local Q&A + demo\ntests/\n  conftest.py     # fixtures: loaded store\n  test_bdd_match_queries.py   # Given/When/Then style\n  test_bdd_team_queries.py\n  test_bdd_player_queries.py\n  test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n  test_bdd_statistics.py\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_dates.py\n  test_server.py   # MCP server tool listing + calling via in-process client? Or call tool functions directly.\npyproject.toml\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md (update)\n```\n\nMCP v2 API: `from mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer`; `@mcp.tool()` decorator; `server.run()` presumably runs stdio. Let me check `run` signature and how tools are invoked in v2, plus how to test with a client in-process (mcp.client.session?). For tests I can just call the underlying tool functions directly (they're plain functions), plus a test that lists tools via the server object. Even better: use the in-memory client from mcp package if available. Let me check what client utilities exist in mcp 2.1.1.\n\nAlso check: does `MCPServer.run()` take transport? And `run_stdio_async`. For the server entry point I'll use `server.run()` — check signature.\n\nKey query capabilities to implement in store:\n\n1. `find_matches(team=None, opponent=None, home_team=None, away_team=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, venue=None, limit=None)` — returns list[Match]. Match has: date (date), datetime, home, away, home_goals, away_goals, competition, source, season, round/stage, extra stats (corners/shots/attacks for BR-Football), stadium (novo), state info.\n\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None)` — all matches between, plus W/D/L counts each, goals.\n\n3. `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)` — matches, W/D/L, GF, GA, win rate, maybe per-competition breakdown.\n\n4. `standings(season, competition='brasileirao-serie-a', source=None)` — computed table with points; champion; relegated (last 4).\n\n5. `players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, order_by='overall', limit)` — search FIFA.\n\n6. `players_at_team(team)` — resolve team → club names in FIFA via canonicalization.\n\n7. `top_scorers` — not inferable (no scorer data), so standings tool covers competition queries; mention in README that top scorer data isn't in datasets.\n\n8. `stats.goals_per_match(competition=...)`, `home_advantage()` (home win %, away win %, draw %), `biggest_wins(competition=None, n=10)`, `team_trends(team)` (per-season W/D/L, GF, GA), `best_records(venue='away'/'home', season=None, min_matches=...)`.\n\n9. Derbies: curated list of famous Brazilian derby pairs (Fla-Flu, Choque-Rei, Majestoso, Clássico dos Milhões, Grenal, Clássico-Rei, Derby Paulista (Palmeiras x Corinthians), Ba-Vi, Atletiba, Clássico Vovô, etc.). `derbies(season=None)` finds matches between those pairs. This addresses \"Show me all derbies in 2023\".\n\nTeam normalization plan (normalize.py):\n\n- `strip_accents(s)`: unicodedata NFKD, remove combining marks; also replace 'ç'→'c' handled by NFKD? 'ç' NFKD = 'c' + combining cedilla — yes.\n- `canonical_key(name)`: lowercase, strip accents, remove punctuation/parenthetical content, remove state suffixes (trailing '-sp', '-rj', etc. or ' sp' forms), remove common legal words (sport club, clube de regatas, esporte clube, etc.), collapse whitespace. Also handle \"Athletico\"/\"Atlético\" spelling variants → map both to \"atletico\"? Careful: \"Athletico-PR\" and \"Atlético-PR\" are the same club (Atlético Paranaense, renamed Athletico in 2019); \"Atlético-GO\" is a different club (Goiás). \"Atlético-MG\" different again. So spelling variants must be contextual. Approach: alias table maps many variants → canonical id. The normalized key for \"athletico-pr\" → strip \"-pr\" → \"athletico\". \"atlético-pr\" → \"atletico\". If I normalize 'athletico' → 'atletico' (h→t? no...). Hmm: map \"athletico\" to \"atletico\" via a spelling-variant folding step: replace 'th' with 't' only for this word? Simpler: after suffix stripping, apply alias dict: {\"athletico\": \"atletico\"}? But \"Atlético-MG\" → \"atletico\" too! Both Athletico-PR and Atlético-MG fold to \"atletico\" — collision!\n\nSo state suffix matters: \"athletico-pr\" vs \"atletico-mg\" vs \"atletico-go\". Keep the state as part of the disambiguation when present. Strategy:\n- Extract trailing state (2-letter UF) if present: e.g. \"Palmeiras-SP\" → base=\"palmeiras\", uf=\"SP\".\n- Then canonicalize base+uf via alias table with entries like (\"athletico\",\"PR\")→\"athletico-pr\" canonical \"Athletico-PR\"; (\"atletico\",\"PR\")→same; (\"atletico\",\"MG\")→\"Atletico-MG\"; (\"atletico\",\"GO\")→\"Atletico-GO\".\n- If no UF: use alias table for ambiguous bases: \"athletico\" → Athletico-PR (only \"Athletico\" spelling w/o suffix appears in Libertadores data: 'Flamengo' vs 'Athletico' final). \"atletico\" alone is ambiguous → could be GO or MG or PR... In Brasileirao_Matches, names are like \"Atletico-GO\", \"Atletico-MG\", \"Athletico-PR\"? Let me check actual team names in each file. Also \"Atletico Goianiense\"? Let me gather all distinct team names across all files to build the alias table properly.\n\nFull names: \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" (spec example), FIFA clubs: \"Atlético Mineiro\", \"Atlético Paranaense\", \"Sport Club do Recife\", \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\", \"Ceará Sporting Club\", \"Grêmio\", \"Santos\", etc.\n\nLet me dump distinct team names from all match files and the FIFA clubs that look Brazilian, then design the alias table. Also note BR-Football names are ASCII-ish (\"Sao Paulo\", \"Flamengo\", \"Athletico Paranaense\", \"Atletico Mineiro\"? let me check).\n\nI'll gather the data now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a9c351e001X1NNWafGOOyagf","sessionID":"ses_fa566b1a7ffetmaN0ZgRQlI0ol","messageID":"msg_05a9b6f58001cGhZoe90WyaEG3"}}
