clubs:\n    k = strip_acc(c).lower()\n    if any(re.search(r'\\b'+b+r'\\b', k) for b in bases):\n        seen.add(c)\nfor c in sorted(seen):\n    print(f'{clubs[c]:4d}  {c}')\nEOF","time":{"start":1788226638282,"end":1788226638307}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the naming landscape well. Key observations:\n\n- Base name + state suffix is the dominant pattern. Base-with-suffix and base-without-suffix both appear (different datasets).\n- \"Guaraní (PAR)\" is a Paraguayan club — parenthetical country tags exist in Libertadores (e.g., \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\"). Note \"Barcelona-EQU\" uses -EQU suffix (3 letters) — my 2-letter UF regex won't catch \"EQU\". For foreign clubs I should treat any trailing \"-XXX\" (2-3 uppercase letters) as country/state tag. But careful: \"Santos-AP\" (Amapá) vs \"Santos-SP\". Both Brazilian states. Good.\n- Tricky collisions when stripping state: \"juventude ma\" vs \"juventude rs\" are different clubs; \"nautico pe\" vs \"nautico rr\"; \"santos ap\" vs \"santos sp\"; \"nacional am\" vs Nacional (URU/MEX?). So when a query comes without state (\"Juventude\"), which is intended? Usually Juventude-RS (Série A regular). The bare \"Juventude\" appears in brf + novo (which is Juventude-RS). So resolution strategy: when the bare base is ambiguous across multiple states, prefer the one that appears as bare base in match datasets (i.e., the more prominent club), else prefer by total match count.\n\nApproach: build a TeamRegistry:\n1. For each distinct raw name across all match files + FIFA clubs + alias table entries, compute (base, uf) via parsing.\n2. canonical_id = f\"{base}-{uf}\" if uf else base. But \"Athletico-PR\" vs \"Atlético-PR\" — same club, spelling differs (\"Athletico\" is the modern name of Atlético Paranaense; older novo data uses \"Atlético-PR\"?? wait: novo has 'Athletico-PR' — from the earlier dump, novo sample teams showed 'Athletico-PR' in 2003. Hmm, actually Athletico-PR appears in novo; and 'atletico pr' key shows bras/cup/lib with both 'Atletico-PR' and 'Atlético - PR' and 'Athletico-PR'? The key collision list shows atletico pr => bras 'Atletico-PR', cup 'Atletico - PR', cup 'Atlético - PR', lib 'Atlético-PR'. And earlier novo listing showed 'Athletico-PR'. Since key() strips accents but not 'th', 'athletico pr' is a separate key from 'atletico pr'. So both spellings exist across files. I need a spelling-variant fold: \"athletico\" → \"atletico\" (safe when combined with state: athletico-pr → atletico-pr; atletico-mg stays distinct).\n\nSo: spelling folds (applied to base): athletico→atletico, \"atletico goianiense\"→? brf might have 'Atletico Goianiense'. Fine as separate club.\n\n- FIFA club names: \"Atlético Mineiro\" (= atletico-mg), \"Atlético Paranaense\" (= atletico-pr), \"Athletic Club MG\"?? saw 'Athletic Club MG' in brf — that's Athletic Club (São João del Rey), different from Atlético-MG. \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → america mg. \"Sport Club do Recife\" → sport pe. \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → ceara. \"Grêmio\" → gremio. \"Santos\" → santos sp (Brazilian; also Santos Laguna is Mexican — different club, \"santos laguna\" stays its own). \"Botafogo\" → botafogo rj (but there's also Botafogo-PB! brf has 'Botafogo PB' presumably). Bare \"Botafogo\" in FIFA = Botafogo-RJ.\n\nSo the registry needs an alias table mapping full/official names → (base, uf):\n- \"atlético mineiro\" → (\"atletico\",\"mg\")\n- \"clube atlético mineiro\" → same\n- \"atlético paranaense\" → (\"atletico\",\"pr\")\n- \"athletico paranaense\" → same\n- \"sport club do recife\" / \"sport recife\" → (\"sport\",\"pe\")\n- \"américa fc (minas gerais)\" / \"américa mineiro\" / \"américa futebol clube (mg)\" → (\"america\",\"mg\")\n- \"ceará sporting club\" → (\"ceara\",\"ce\")\n- \"sport club corinthians paulista\" → (\"corinthians\",\"sp\")\n- \"club de regatas vasco da gama\" / \"vasco da gama\" → (\"vasco\",\"rj\")\n- \"grêmio fbpa\" / \"grêmio foot-ball porto alegrense\" → (\"gremio\",\"rs\")\n- \"são paulo fc\" → (\"sao paulo\",\"sp\")\n- \"fluminense fc\" → (\"fluminense\",\"rj\")\n- \"crf flamengo\" / \"clube de regatas do flamengo\" → (\"flamengo\",\"rj\")\n- \"botafogo fr\" → (\"botafogo\",\"rj\")\n- \"santos fc\" → (\"santos\",\"sp\")\n- \"sc internacional\" / \"internacional rs\" → (\"internacional\",\"rs\")\n- \"bahia\" → (\"bahia\",\"ba\")  [already fine]\n- \"vitoria ba\" / \"esporte clube vitória\" → (\"vitoria\",\"ba\")\n- \"goiás ec\" → goias\n- \"fortaleza ec\" → fortaleza\n- \"coritiba fc\" → coritiba\n- \"paraná clube\" → parana\n- \"chapecoense\" → chapecoense\n- \"avai fc\" → avai\n- \"criciuma\" etc.\n\nAlso state-name maps for FIFA clubs that include state as word: \"America MG\", \"Gremio RS\" (brf style). My key parser already converts \"-MG\" and \" MG\" suffix handling? Currently regex handles \"-sp\" → \" sp\" but \"America MG\" naturally gives \"america mg\". Good. But bare \"Ceara\"/\"Gremio\" vs \"Ceara CE\": canonical id must merge these. So canonicalization: after (base, uf) extraction:\n- If uf present: canonical = (base, uf) — but if base alone is a known distinct club with a *unique* state, merge to that.\n- If uf absent: canonical = (base, preferred_uf) if unambiguous or if base is famous enough; else base alone.\n\nSimplest robust approach: compute for each base the set of ufs seen across all datasets (weighted by match counts + alias table). Then:\n- canonical(base, uf) = (base, uf) if uf else (base, dominant_uf(base)) if dominant exists and base isn't a foreign club.\n- Merging \"palmeiras sp\" and \"palmeiras\" → both canonicalize to (\"palmeiras\", \"sp\")? But wait — is bare \"palmeiras\" always Palmeiras-SP? In the datasets yes (brf/lib/novo are Brazilian clubs in those contexts; Libertadores \"Palmeiras\" = Palmeiras-SP; \"Santos\" in lib = Santos-SP; \"Guarani\" in novo = Guarani-SP? novo 'Guarani' — Campinas-SP club. But lib 'Guaraní (PAR)' is Paraguayan — has country tag so parses to base 'guarani' with uf 'par'... I'll treat 3-letter suffixes as foreign markers and canonical = \"guarani-par\" (distinct from guarani-sp). Hmm but my current regex only strips 2-letter. I'll extend to 2-3 letter trailing tokens after '-' when they're uppercase in the raw name or in a known set of country codes. Simpler: treat trailing token after '-' as suffix if len 2 (Brazilian UF) or len 3 (country code) — but careful with names like \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" (suffix RJ, fine) and... \"Aguia Negra-MS\" fine. Any club whose base contains '-' without being a suffix? e.g. \"Vila Nova-GO\"? that's a suffix. \"Atletico-MG\". I think len2/len3 uppercase-suffix heuristic works. But note \"Barcelona-EQU\" — 'EQU' 3 letters. Also \"Guaraní (PAR)\" — parenthetical. Also Libertadores might have \"The Strongest (BOL)\" etc.\n\nAlso FIFA \"Paraná\" → parana-pr (Paraná Clube). But bare \"parana\" in brf = Paraná. OK dominant uf = pr.\n\nAmbiguous bases where dominant_uf may be wrong:\n- \"juventude\": Juventude-RS (Série A) vs Juventude-MA (Maranhão). Bare 'juventude' appears in brf+novo = RS. Dominant → rs. \n- \"nautico\": Nautico-PE vs Nautico-RR. Bare? not seen; bras/cup have nautico pe; brf nautico rr. Count PE > RR probably. Dominant → pe. \n- \"nacional\": Nacional-AM vs Nacional (URU/MEX). Bare 'Nacional' might appear in brf as Nacional-AM? or 'Nacional AM'. If ambiguous and counts are close, keep base-only canonical (separate clubs). Hmm, risky to auto-merge. My rule: if a base has multiple ufs and no alias-table override, then (base, None) canonicalizes to just base (no uf), and it only matches bare occurrences. But queries for \"Nacional\" then wouldn't match Nacional-AM. Edge case, acceptable — or better: alias table pins \"nacional\" → nacional-am? In Brazilian context Nacional-AM is the Série C club. I'll pin via alias table where confident: juventude→rs, nautico→pe, santos→sp, nacional→am? Let me check brf 'Nacional' bare... I saw 'Nacional AM' in brf. And 'Nacional (URU)' in lib. Let me just check quickly what bare names exist and their uf-variants. I'll do a final registry build and eyeball ambiguous bases (those with >1 uf).\n\nFIFA club matching: canonicalize FIFA club names through the same registry; FIFA club \"Santos\" → santos-sp; \"Santos Laguna\" → \"santos laguna\" (own canonical, no uf). Query \"Santos\" → canonical santos-sp → FIFA clubs canonicalized to santos-sp match. \"Atlético Mineiro\" → atletico-mg. \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → need alias \"america fc (minas gerais)\" → america mg (my parser strips parenthetical → \"america fc\" → base \"america fc\"; alias needed: \"america fc\" → america-mg? but \"América FC (Natal)\" might exist too... let me check FIFA club names containing 'América'. Also \"Sport Club do Recife\" → alias. I'll dump all FIFA clubs that appear Brazilian-ish or contain Brazilian city/team words and build the alias table from that. Let me get the full distinct FIFA club list that contains known base names (accent-stripped containment).\n\nActually, a cleaner approach for FIFA→team matching: for each FIFA club, canonicalize; if its canonical (base,uf) matches a team seen in match data → link. For the alias table, I need entries for FIFA-style full names. Let me dump relevant FIFA clubs now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a9d1104001y3y4dnZNCKRvrW","sessionID":"ses_fa566b1a7ffetmaN0ZgRQlI0ol","messageID":"msg_05a9c36d30019bqU3H8Il1lhOy"}}
