{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788226725769,"sessionID":"ses_fa566b1a7ffetmaN0ZgRQlI0ol","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_b0e6afef12aa4689844a9491","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"source venv/bin/activate && python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\n\ndef load(path):\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nfifa = load('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv')\nfor pat in ['Gabriel Barbosa','Neymar','Gabigol','Alisson','Casemiro']:\n    hits = [ (r['Name'], r['Club'], r['Overall'], r['Position'], r['Nationality']) for r in fifa if pat.lower() in r['Name'].lower()]\n    print(pat, '->', hits[:3])\n\n# 2019 Serie A standings from novo\nnovo = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\npts = {}\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nst = defaultdict(lambda: [0,0,0,0,0])  # p,w,d,l,gf,ga\nfor r in novo:\n    if r['Ano'] != '2019': continue\n    h,a,hg,ag = r['Equipe_mandante'], r['Equipe_visitante'], int(r['Gols_mandante']), int(r['Gols_visitante'])\n    for t in (h,a): \n        if t not in st: st[t]=[0]*6\n    st[h][4]+=hg; st[h][5]+=ag; st[a][4]+=ag; st[a][5]+=hg\n    if hg>ag: st[h][0]+=3; st[h][1]+=1; st[a][3]+=1\n    elif ag>hg: st[a][0]+=3; st[a][1]+=1; st[h][3]+=1\n    else:\n        st[h][0]+=1; st[a][0]+=1; st[h][2]+=1; st[a][2]+=1\ntable = sorted(st.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1][0], -(kv[1][1]), -(kv[1][4]-kv[1][5])))\nprint('\\n2019 top3:', [(t, v[0]) for t,v in table[:3]])\nprint('2019 bottom4:', [(t, v[0]) for t,v in table[-4:]])\n\n# Flamengo x Fluminense in novo+bras\ncnt=0\nfor r in novo:\n    if {r['Equipe_mandante'],r['Equipe_visitante']}=={'Flamengo','Fluminense'}: cnt+=1\nprint('\\nFla-Flu in novo:', cnt)\n\nbras = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\ndef strip_acc(s):\n    import unicodedata\n    return ''.join(c for c in unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', s) if not unicodedata.combining(c))\ncnt2=0\nfor r in bras:\n    if {strip_acc(r['home_team']).split('-')[0], strip_acc(r['away_team']).split('-')[0]}=={'Flamengo','Fluminense'}: cnt2+=1\nprint('Fla-Flu in bras:', cnt2)\nEOF"},"output":"Gabriel Barbosa -> []\nNeymar -> [('Neymar Jr', 'Paris Saint-Germain', '92', 'LW', 'Brazil')]\nGabigol -> []\nAlisson -> [('Alisson', 'Liverpool', '85', 'GK', 'Brazil')]\nCasemiro -> [('Casemiro', 'Real Madrid', '88', 'CDM', 'Brazil')]\n\n2019 top3: [('Flamengo', 90), ('Santos', 74), ('Palmeiras', 74)]\n2019 bottom4: [('Cruzeiro', 36), ('CSA', 32), ('Chapecoense', 32), ('Avaí', 20)]\n\nFla-Flu in novo: 34\nFla-Flu in bras: 22\n","metadata":{"output":"Gabriel Barbosa -> []\nNeymar -> [('Neymar Jr', 'Paris Saint-Germain', '92', 'LW', 'Brazil')]\nGabigol -> []\nAlisson -> [('Alisson', 'Liverpool', '85', 'GK', 'Brazil')]\nCasemiro -> [('Casemiro', 'Real Madrid', '88', 'CDM', 'Brazil')]\n\n2019 top3: [('Flamengo', 90), ('Santos', 74), ('Palmeiras', 74)]\n2019 bottom4: [('Cruzeiro', 36), ('CSA', 32), ('Chapecoense', 32), ('Avaí', 20)]\n\nFla-Flu in novo: 34\nFla-Flu in bras: 22\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"source venv/bin/activate && python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\n\ndef load(path):\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nfifa = load('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv')\nfor pat in ['Gabriel Barbosa','Neymar','Gabigol','Alisson','Casemiro']:\n    hits = [ (r['Name'], r['Club'], r['Overall'], r['Position'], r['Nationality']) for r in fifa if pat.lower() in r['Name'].lower()]\n    print(pat, '->', hits[:3])\n\n# 2019 Serie A standings from novo\nnovo = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\npts = {}\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nst = defaultdict(lambda: [0,0,0,0,0])  # p,w,d,l,gf,ga\nfor r in novo:\n    if r['Ano'] != '2019': continue\n    h,a,hg,ag = r['Equipe_mandante'], r['Equipe_visitante'], int(r['Gols_mandante']), int(r['Gols_visitante'])\n    for t in (h,a): \n        if t not in st: st[t]=[0]*6\n    st[h][4]+=hg; st[h][5]+=ag; st[a][4]+=ag; st[a][5]+=hg\n    if hg>ag: st[h][0]+=3; st[h][1]+=1; st[a][3]+=1\n    elif ag>hg: st[a][0]+=3; st[a][1]+=1; st[h][3]+=1\n    else:\n        st[h][0]+=1; st[a][0]+=1; st[h][2]+=1; st[a][2]+=1\ntable = sorted(st.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1][0], -(kv[1][1]), -(kv[1][4]-kv[1][5])))\nprint('\\n2019 top3:', [(t, v[0]) for t,v in table[:3]])\nprint('2019 bottom4:', [(t, v[0]) for t,v in table[-4:]])\n\n# Flamengo x Fluminense in novo+bras\ncnt=0\nfor r in novo:\n    if {r['Equipe_mandante'],r['Equipe_visitante']}=={'Flamengo','Fluminense'}: cnt+=1\nprint('\\nFla-Flu in novo:', cnt)\n\nbras = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\ndef strip_acc(s):\n    import unicodedata\n    return ''.join(c for c in unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', s) if not unicodedata.combining(c))\ncnt2=0\nfor r in bras:\n    if {strip_acc(r['home_team']).split('-')[0], strip_acc(r['away_team']).split('-')[0]}=={'Flamengo','Fluminense'}: cnt2+=1\nprint('Fla-Flu in bras:', cnt2)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788226725744,"end":1788226725768}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have the FIFA club landscape. Brazilian clubs in FIFA data: América FC (Minas Gerais), Atlético Mineiro, Atlético Paranaense, Bahia, Botafogo, Ceará Sporting Club, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Grêmio, Internacional, Paraná, Santos, Sport Club do Recife, Vitória. Plus from the earlier \"top clubs of Brazilians\": Flamengo? Wait — \"top clubs of Brazilians\" didn't show Flamengo, Palmeiras, Corinthians, São Paulo, Vasco, Atlético Mineiro... it showed Grêmio, Atlético Mineiro (yes), Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Internacional, América FC (MG), Botafogo, Bahia, Paraná, Atlético Paranaense, Vitória, Sport Club do Recife, Chapecoense, Ceará. So Flamengo/Palmeiras/Corinthians/São Paulo/Vasco are NOT in this FIFA dataset (it's FIFA 19-era Brazilian clubs limited to those licensed). That's fine — the tool should report which Brazilian clubs exist in data.\n\nForeign clubs to be careful about: América de Cali, Club América, Atlético Nacional, Atlético Madrid, Santos Laguna, Vitória Guimarães, Vitória de Setúbal. My canonical approach handles these: \"Santos Laguna\" → canonical \"santos laguna\" ≠ \"santos sp\". \"Atlético Nacional\" → \"atletico nacional\" (Colombian) — bare base \"atletico nacional\", no uf, distinct from \"atletico mg/pr/go\". Good. \"Vitória\" (Brazilian, BA) vs \"Vitória Guimarães\" — \"Vitória\" bare → alias to vitoria-ba. Good.\n\nNow, the alias table design:\n\n```python\nALIASES = {\n    # canonical -> list of alias keys (normalized, accent-stripped)\n}\n```\n\nI'll implement `TeamRegistry` in normalize.py:\n\n1. `parse_name(raw)` → (base, suffix): strip accents, lowercase, remove parentheticals (but detect country code inside parens), remove common filler words (\"sport club\", \"esporte clube\", \"clube de regatas\", \"futebol clube\", \"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"sp fc\"...), extract trailing state/country token (2-letter UF list of 27 Brazilian states + DC? just known UFs; 3-letter foreign codes from lib data), fold \"athletico\"→\"atletico\".\n2. Canonical id: `(base, uf)` if uf else `base` (later resolved via dominance).\n3. ALIAS table maps explicit keys → (base, uf) for full names.\n4. Registry built from all data: base → Counter of ufs. `resolve(raw)` → canonical id string like \"atletico-mg\". For bare bases with exactly one uf observed → merge. With multiple ufs → use ALIAS pinned default or the most frequent; if ambiguous and no clear winner (e.g., equal), keep bare (won't match suffixed ones)... Actually simpler: always pick most frequent uf, tie-break by alias table priority order. For \"nacional\": ufs = am (brf + cup nacional-am), uru/par/mex from lib with country codes... wait those have country suffixes (URU), (MEX), (PAR) → canonical nacional-uru etc. distinct. Bare \"Nacional\"? If none, base \"nacional\" resolves to nacional-am. OK.\n\nDisplay names: keep a display name per canonical id — the most common raw name, preferring accented forms. I'll pick most frequent raw name, tie → longest? e.g., for \"gremio-rs\" raws: 'Grêmio' (brf/lib/novo), 'Gremio-RS' (bras), 'Gremio RS' (brf), 'Grêmio - RS' (cup). Most frequent: 'Grêmio' (3 datasets worth of matches, brf has many Serie A/B/C matches though). Counts by matches: brf gremio ~ hundreds. Either way \"Grêmio\" is fine display. For canonical display I might prefer explicit \"Grêmio (RS)\"? Keep simple: display = most frequent raw variant.\n\nActually for output quality, I'll display canonical as the base name (title-cased from most common raw), with uf appended only when needed for disambiguation (i.e., when the base has multiple ufs in registry: e.g., \"América (MG)\" vs \"América (RN)\"). Hmm, but for correctness in lists (standings), showing \"América-MG\" style is clearer. I'll display `Base-UF` when uf known and base is ambiguous across ufs; else just `Base`. E.g. Palmeiras → \"Palmeiras\"; América-MG → \"América-MG\". Wait, but standings example shows \"Flamengo\", \"Santos\", \"Palmeiras\" — matches this approach. But when I merge \"palmeiras sp\" + \"palmeiras\" → canonical \"palmeiras-sp\"? If canonical id includes uf always (when known), display drops uf if unambiguous. That's clean: canonical id = (base, uf) always where uf known; display name = base if base maps to single uf else base-uf. For foreign: display base + country tag maybe. Keep it simple.\n\nWait, there's subtlety: base \"santos\" has ufs {sp, ap} → ambiguous → display \"Santos-SP\" for santos-sp and \"Santos-AP\" for santos-ap. Good. Base \"palmeiras\" → only sp → display \"Palmeiras\". \n\nCompetition model:\n- source files map to competitions:\n  - Brasileirao_Matches.csv → competition \"Brasileirão Série A\", source \"brasileirao\", seasons 2012-2022\n  - novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv → same competition \"Brasileirão Série A\", source \"brasileirao_historico\", seasons 2003-2019\n  - Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv → \"Copa do Brasil\", source \"copa_do_brasil\", 2012-2021\n  - Libertadores_Matches.csv → \"Copa Libertadores\", source \"libertadores\", 2013-2022\n  - BR-Football-Dataset.csv → tournament column directly (\"Serie A\", \"Serie B\", \"Serie C\", \"Copa do Brasil\"), source \"br_football\". Serie A here = \"Brasileirão Série A\" too (2014-2023) — but careful: overlapping seasons with Brasileirao_Matches for 2014-2022 → duplicate matches if querying \"all matches between X and Y\" across sources. Spec says \"Head-to-head in dataset\". I'll include a `source` filter param, and default behavior for head-to-head: use all sources but dedupe exact duplicate matches (same date + teams + score)? Duplicates across bras & brf for Serie A same season same date same teams — dates might differ slightly (brf date only has date, bras datetime). Dedupe key: (date, home_canonical, away_canonical, competition). That would merge bras/brf Serie A duplicates and keep distinct competitions separate. novo vs bras overlap 2012-2019 Serie A! Same competition same matches. Dedupe by (date, home, away, competition) merges those too. \n\nBut wait — novo and bras dates: novo Data = DD/MM/YYYY; bras datetime = \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\". Same match → same date. Yes dedupe works. But careful: round numbers might differ; keep first occurrence (prefer bras source? order sources: brasileirao, brasileirao_historico, copa, libertadores, br_football — for Serie A duplicates, bras preferred).\n\nHmm, but for standings, dedupe matters a lot: if I compute 2019 Serie A standings from both novo and bras and brf, I'd double-count. With dedupe by (date, home, away, competition), duplicates collapse. Two-legged cup ties on same date? impossible (different dates). Replays? Copa do Brasil has no replays in this era. Fine.\n\nEdge: same teams same date in different competition (e.g., friendly + league) — competition is in the key so fine.\n\nSo the unified Match store: all matches from all files, each with competition canonical name, season, round/stage, venue, date, optional time, goals (may be None for unplayed), source, plus stats (corners/shots/attacks/ht result when available). Dedupe for queries by default with `dedupe=True` param. Each match gets stable id. For dedupe, keep the richest record (one with stats or round info). I'll implement: when adding, if key exists, keep existing but merge missing fields (e.g., add venue/stats from novo/brf twin). Simplest: prefer by source priority, then merge optional fields.\n\nCompetitions (canonical):\n- \"Brasileirão Série A\" (sources: brasileirao, brasileirao_historico, br_football where tournament=='Serie A')\n- \"Brasileirão Série B\" (br_football)\n- \"Brasileirão Série C\" (br_football)\n- \"Copa do Brasil\" (cup + br_football)\n- \"Copa Libertadores\" (libertadores)\n\nCompetition matching for query param: accept fuzzy: \"brasileirao\", \"serie a\", \"série a\", \"copa do brasil\", \"libertadores\", etc. via normalized containment. I'll write a `match_competition(query)` helper. Also `source` param filters exact source.\n\nStandings: for Série A seasons, compute from deduped matches of competition. Champion = top points; tie-break: wins, goal diff, goals for (approximation). Relegated = bottom 4 (for recent seasons; 2020 had 4 relegated; 2003-2005 different but fine, we just report bottom 4 and label \"relegation zone\"). Points: win=3, draw=1 (post-1994 rules; all data is 2003+, fine).\n\nNow the MCP tools. Design tool list (natural-language-facing, returns formatted text + structured data). MCP v2 `MCPServer.tool()` decorator. Tools:\n\n1. `search_matches(team, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, venue=None, limit=20)` → list of matches + count.\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, limit=None)` → matches + summary W/D/L.\n3. `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)` → record breakdown (overall/home/away), goals, competitions played.\n4. `team_season_history(team)` → per-season records (trend).\n5. `standings(season, competition=\"Brasileirão Série A\")` → table + champion + relegated.\n6. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_age=None, order_by=\"overall\", limit=20)` → players.\n7. `team_players(team)` → players at club matching team (cross-file: FIFA club ↔ match team).\n8. `top_brazilian_players(club=None, position=None, limit=10)`.\n9. `competition_info(competition=None, season=None)` → seasons covered, match counts, teams count per competition (what data exists).\n10. `derbies(season=None, competition=None)` → matches between curated derby pairs.\n11. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, n=10)`.\n12. `goals_analysis(competition=None, season=None)` → avg goals/match, home win rate, away win rate, draw rate, biggest wins.\n13. `best_records(venue=None, season=None, competition=None, min_matches=10)` → team ranking by points/win rate.\n14. `compare_teams(team_a, team_b)` → side-by-side stats + H2H.\n15. `find_team(name)` → resolve team name → canonical, aliases, datasets where present, Brazilian club matching in FIFA. (Helps \"Who is X?\" team identity.)\n16. Maybe `last_match_between(team_a, team_b)` — covered by search_matches sorted desc; but simple lookup Q \"When did Flamengo last play Corinthians?\" — head_to_head with limit returns most recent first. I'll sort search results desc by date by default with limit; provide `order` param.\n\nThat's a lot of tools but each is simple. Maybe consolidate: `team_players` can be `search_players(club=team)` — but the club resolution via team canonicalization is special; I'll make search_players use team-canonical resolution for club param too. Keep `team_players` as convenience? I'll fold it into search_players (club param accepts team names) — cleaner. And `top_brazilian_players` = search_players(nationality='Brazil', order_by overall, limit). Also fold. Final tool list (12):\n\n1. find_team\n2. search_matches\n3. head_to_head\n4. team_stats\n5. team_season_history (trends)\n6. standings\n7. search_players (name/nationality/club/position/rating filters; club accepts team names)\n8. competition_info\n9. derbies\n10. biggest_wins\n11. goals_analysis\n12. best_records\n13. compare_teams\n\n13 tools. Each returns dict with a formatted \"summary\" text plus structured lists — good for LLM consumption. Actually MCP tools returning strings is most robust; I'll return a JSON-serializable dict with \"summary\" (formatted text like the spec examples) + \"data\" (structured). MCP v2 supports structured output via pydantic-annotated returns; returning dict should be fine (it'll serialize). Let me keep return type `dict` and check the server serializes it. I'll verify with a quick in-process test using mcp client.\n\nTests: BDD-style with pytest, given/when/then comments/structure:\n- test_normalize.py — name variations (\"Palmeiras-SP\" == \"Palmeiras\" == \"palmeiras\"; \"Athletico-PR\" == \"Atlético-PR\"; \"Santos\" ≠ \"Santos-AP\" ≠ \"Santos Laguna\"; \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" → Corinthians; FIFA full names).\n- test_dates.py — ISO, Brazilian, datetime, NA.\n- test_loaders.py — all 6 files load, counts match (4180, 1337, 1255, 10296, 6886, 18207).\n- test_bdd_match_queries.py — Gherkin-style scenarios: matches between Flamengo & Fluminense (each has date, scores, competition); matches by season; by competition; date range; Flamengo vs Fluminense H2H counts.\n- test_bdd_team_queries.py — Palmeiras 2023 stats (W/D/L/goals); Corinthians home record 2022; best team goals Serie A 2023; H2H compare.\n- test_bdd_player_queries.py — Gabriel Barbosa lookup (check he exists in FIFA data! FIFA 19-era — Gabigol at Flamengo? Flamengo not in club list! Gabigol may be at Inter Milan or Flamengo. Let me check), Brazilian players count, club players, São Paulo forwards (São Paulo not in clubs... need to check!). \n\nHmm wait — the spec examples say \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" and \"Show me all forwards from São Paulo FC\" — but if FIFA data lacks Flamengo/São Paulo clubs, those queries return empty. Let me verify which Brazilian clubs exist in FIFA data. From the dump: América FC (MG), Atlético Mineiro, Atlético Paranaense, Bahia, Botafogo, Ceará, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Grêmio, Internacional, Paraná, Santos, Sport Recife, Vitória. So Flamengo, Palmeiras, Corinthians, São Paulo, Vasco are absent. The tools should handle this gracefully (\"No Flamengo squad in FIFA dataset; Brazilian clubs available: ...\"). Tests should use clubs that exist (e.g., Grêmio, Santos) and also assert graceful empty results for Flamengo. Let me check Gabriel Barbosa in the data.\n\n- test_bdd_competition_queries.py — 2019 Brasileirão winner = Flamengo (verify from novo data standings); standings shape; relegated 2020; competition_info contents; Copa do Brasil finals (2012-2021 rounds).\n- test_bdd_statistics.py — avg goals per match Série A ~2.4ish; home win rate > away win rate; biggest wins list; best away records.\n- test_server.py — server exposes the tools; call a tool through MCP client session in-memory.\n\nLet me now verify a few data facts to use in tests:\n1. Gabigol / Neymar in fifa_data.\n2. 2019 standings from novo: Flamengo champion.\n3. Flamengo-Fluminense H2H counts.\n4. Biggest win in data.\n\nThen write the code. Also CLI (`cli.py`) for demo + a `demo.py`? CLI with subcommands mapping to tools is nice for manual verification. And `run_server.py` / `python -m brasil_mcp.server`.\n\nNote the mcp v2 API details: check `MCPServer.tool()` decorator usage and how `run()` works. Let me inspect quickly. Also in-memory client: `mcp.client.session.ClientSession` with memory streams? Check available client utilities.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a9e6693001BP3N9OB3yrVhJe","sessionID":"ses_fa566b1a7ffetmaN0ZgRQlI0ol","messageID":"msg_05a9d133c001XhUfq1eaOqEBOu"}}
