{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788227018801,"sessionID":"ses_fa566b1a7ffetmaN0ZgRQlI0ol","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_d6479ff04cc4410397a5f2e5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=68\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"brasil-mcp\"\nversion = \"1.0.0\"\ndescription = \"MCP server providing a knowledge-graph interface for Brazilian soccer data\"\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.10\"\nlicense = { text = \"MIT\" }\ndependencies = [\"mcp>=2.1\"]\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\ndev = [\"pytest>=8\", \"pytest-cov\"]\n\n[project.scripts]\nbrasil-soccer-mcp = \"brasil_mcp.server:main\"\nbrasil-soccer = \"brasil_mcp.cli:main\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\ntestpaths = [\"tests\"]\naddopts = \"-q\"\n","filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-0bivsr25/retort-ce22193d1e90/pyproject.toml"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-0bivsr25/retort-ce22193d1e90/pyproject.toml","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"pyproject.toml","time":{"start":1788227018792,"end":1788227018799}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I can see the low-level server run pattern:\n\n```python\nasync with create_client_server_memory_streams() as (client_streams, server_streams):\n    async with ClientSession(*client_streams) as session:\n        # run server concurrently\n        async def run_server():\n            await server._lowlevel_server.run(server_streams[0], server_streams[1], server._lowlevel_server.create_initialization_options())\n        async with anyio.create_task_group() as tg:\n            tg.start_soon(run_server)\n            result = await session.initialize()\n            tools = await session.list_tools()\n            res = await session.call_tool('search_matches', {'team': 'Flamengo'})\n```\n\nAlso need to check the `tool()` decorator in v2 — how it handles function signatures (sync vs async, pydantic validation). Let me write a quick smoke test of the whole pattern before writing the full server. Actually let me just write the real code now and iterate. First pyproject and the package modules.\n\nLet me write the files now. Start with pyproject.toml, then the modules in dependency order: dates, normalize, models, loaders, store, queries, server, cli.\n\nOne design point for `find_matches` defaults: sort by date desc, limit default 50, and return total count so we can say \"... (15 more matches in dataset)\".\n\nHead-to-head summary format per spec:\n```\nHead-to-head in dataset: Flamengo 12 wins, Fluminense 8 wins, 7 draws\n```\n\nteam_stats format per spec example. standings per spec example.\n\nNow — the JSON-serializability of Match: I'll add `Match.to_dict()` and `Player.to_dict()`.\n\nAlso spec success criterion: \"Cross-file queries work (e.g., player + match data)\" — my `search_players` with club=team-name bridges FIFA ↔ match teams; also `find_team` reports both match-data presence AND FIFA squad. And `compare_teams` could include squads? Keep compare_teams: stats from match data + top players from FIFA at each club. That's a nice cross-file query. \n\nLet me now write everything.\n\nnormalize.py details — DEFAULT_UF pins. Let me define after seeing registry build. I'll write the module with a `TEAM_ALIASES` dict and `DEFAULT_UF` dict, then a `TeamRegistry` class:\n\n```python\nclass TeamRegistry:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._observations = {}  # raw -> count\n        self._base_ufs = {}      # base -> Counter(uf)\n        self._canonical_raws = {}  # canonical -> Counter(raw)\n        self._alias = {}         # normalized full raw -> canonical\n    def observe(self, raw, count=1): parse and record\n    def finalize / resolve(raw) -> canonical id or None\n    def display(canonical) -> str\n    def known_teams() -> list canonical\n    def search(name) -> suggestions\n```\n\nResolution of unknown query: exact parse → canonical by rules; if base unseen → None (unknown). Suggestions: normalized containment over known raws/bases.\n\nEdge: query \"Flamengo\" parse → base flamengo, no uf → base_ufs['flamengo'] = {'RJ': n} → flamengo-rj ✓. Query \"Palmeiras-SP\" → palmeiras-sp ✓. Query \"palmeiras sp\" → tokens [palmeiras, sp] trailing 'sp' UF → palmeiras-sp ✓. Query \"SPORT CLUB CORINTHIANS PAULISTA\" → parse: tokens [sport, club, corinthians, paulista] → alias hit? I'll add pipeline result: trailing adjective paulista → uf SP, then tokens [sport, club, corinthians] filter → corinthians → corinthians-sp ✓. Query \"São Paulo FC\" → [sao, paulo, fc] → no uf suffix ('paulo' not UF)... wait 'SP' is the UF, 'paulo' is not in UF set. trailing adjective? 'paulo' no. filter fc → 'sao paulo' base → registry: sao paulo → SP → sao-paulo-sp ✓.\n\nWait, issue: UF tokens are 2-letter — any 2-letter trailing token that equals a UF gets stripped. 'Go' — no club ends with 'go' word. 'Ec'? filtered before? Order: suffix extraction happens before filler filter. 'Atletico GO' (brf style 'Atletico GO'?) — brf uses 'Atletico GO'? It uses 'America MG', 'Gremio RS' style — 'GO' = Goiás UF for Atletico-GO ✓. But e.g. hypothetical 'Xx EC'? no 2-letter issue. What about 'Inter de Limeira'? no. OK.\n\nBUT: what about club names with 2-letter last word that's NOT a state? e.g., 'Nautico RR' RR is state ✓. I think fine.\n\nCareful with 'Bahia de Feira' — tokens [bahia, de, feira] → no uf. base 'bahia de feira'. Filter 'de' prepositions? If I filter {'de','da','do','dos','das'}, 'bahia de feira' → 'bahia feira' — changes base but consistently across datasets (both would normalize same way). Filtering prepositions is fine as long as consistent. But 'Vasco da Gama' → 'vasco gama'!! alias 'vasco da gama' → vasco-rj handles exact, but 'Vasco da Gama-RJ' from bras → tokens [vasco, da, gama, rj] → uf rj, [vasco, da, gama] → filter 'da' → 'vasco gama' ✗ inconsistent with alias! So DON'T filter prepositions. Only filter legal-entity words: sport?? no (Sport club). Let me choose FILLER = {'club','clube','esporte','regatas','futebol','athletic','fc','ec','sc','ac','cr','fr','ecb'} and NOT 'sport' (too dangerous for Sport-PE; instead alias 'sport club corinthians paulista' & 'sport club do recife' explicitly; generic 'Sport Club X' → tokens [sport, club, x] → filter club → 'sport x' — e.g., 'Sport Club Gaúcho'? base 'sport gaico'... whatever, rare). Hmm wait — if 'sport' isn't filler, 'Esporte Clube Vitória' → [esporte, clube, vitoria] → filter → 'vitoria' ✓. 'Boavista Sport Club ... - RJ' → [boavista, sport, club] → filter 'club' only → 'boavista sport' — base 'boavista sport'. Consistent within datasets (cup only). OK.\n\nActually wait, there's also 'America FC Natal' (brf) → [america, fc, natal] → filter fc → 'america natal' → base 'america natal' with no uf — different base than 'america'! Then FIFA 'América FC (Minas Gerais)' → 'america minas gerais' → adjective/state extraction 'minas gerais' → uf MG + base 'america' → america-mg ✓. And 'America RN' → america-rn. 'América-MG' → america-mg ✓. But 'america natal' stays its own base... America FC Natal = América-RN! Inconsistent: cup 'América - RN' → america-rn, brf 'America FC Natal' → 'america natal' — same club, different canonicals. Add alias: 'america fc natal' → ('america','RN'). Also 'America FC Natal' — hmm what about 'America FC (Natal)'? Not in FIFA. Add alias entries for such city-qualified names where the club is notable. Let me scan brf names containing 'fc' or city words to find these cases... This could rabbit-hole; the notable Série A/B clubs matter most. Let me scan for bases that differ but share first token + are likely same club. I'll handle via aliases for the ones I find in a quick scan of distinct names. Let me do that scan after writing normalize.py — print registry summary and eyeball.\n\nTime to write code. I'll be pragmatic: write modules, then a scratch script to dump registry stats (ambiguous bases, distinct canonicals per base) and tune aliases.\n\npyproject.toml:\n\n```toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=68\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"brasil-mcp\"\nversion = \"1.0.0\"\ndescription = \"MCP server providing a knowledge-graph interface for Brazilian soccer data\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.10\"\ndependencies = [\"mcp>=2.1\"]\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\ndev = [\"pytest>=8\", \"pytest-cov\"]\n\n[project.scripts]\nbrasil-soccer-mcp = \"brasil_mcp.server:main\"\nbrasil-soccer = \"brasil_mcp.cli:main\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\ntestpaths = [\"tests\"]\naddopts = \"-q\"\n```\n\nNote: mcp>=2.1 requires Python 3.10+; we have 3.12 ✓.\n\nNow writing the code files. I'll aim for high-quality, well-documented (docstrings not comments — the user's prompt says \"DO NOT ADD ANY COMMENTS unless asked\"... but the prompts.txt sample mentions \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — that's the SAMPLE prompt variation, not my instructions. My TASK.md doesn't ask for comments. The system prompt says don't add comments unless asked. But docstrings are customary and the code-production quality demands reasonable docs. The instruction says \"DO NOT ADD ***ANY*** COMMENTS unless asked\". I'll use docstrings only (they're not technically comments and are needed for MCP tool descriptions), keep inline comments out. MCP tool descriptions come from docstrings — those are essential for the MCP server. Good compromise: module docstrings + function docstrings, zero inline comments.\n\nLet me write all files now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05aa2e024001HQjgzL56zd0LEK","sessionID":"ses_fa566b1a7ffetmaN0ZgRQlI0ol","messageID":"msg_05aa218b9001esSGMJQo2b1VN1"}}
