only for the specific adjectives that are NEVER part of a club name... 'cearense' IS part of 'Atlético Cearense' and 'Ceará Sporting'... Let me check which adjectives appear as trailing tokens in actual data: 'paulista' (Corinthians Paulista alias), 'mineiro' (Atletico Mineiro alias), 'paranaense' (alias). Any OTHERS in the data? Probably not. So: restrict adjective handling to alias table only (add 'corinthians paulista', 'atletico mineiro', 'atletico paranaense', 'atletico goiano'? etc.) and REMOVE the general adjective extraction. The state-NAME words ('minas gerais' etc.) extraction: keep? 'América FC (Minas Gerais)' — parens stripped, so tokens never contain 'minas gerais'. FIFA club names with state names unparenthesized? Don't think so. Keep the state-name extraction (harmless, helps queries like 'America Minas Gerais').\n\nSo the plan for normalize.py v2:\n1. Remove `_extract_adjective` call (keep function? remove entirely).\n2. Reorder: tokens → uf/country extraction → alias lookup (on joined tokens, i.e., base WITHOUT uf) → legal filter → spelling fold → alias lookup again → base.\n   Also alias lookup on FULL string first (covers multi-word aliases like 'sport club do recife' where nothing is extracted — no uf there anyway).\n   Simplest implementation: \n   ```\n   def _alias_for(tokens): \n       return TEAM_ALIASES.get(' '.join(tokens))\n   ```\n   Steps: full = tokens (before uf extraction) — alias_for(full) hits 'vasco da gama rj'? no... hmm 'Vasco Da Gama RJ' (brf) → tokens [vasco, da, gama, rj] → uf-strip rj → [vasco, da, gama] → alias 'vasco da gama' ✓ hit.\n   'Sport Club do Recife' → no uf → [sport, club, do, recife] → alias ✓ hit.\n   After legal filter: [sport, do, recife] → alias miss → continue.\n3. Add aliases: 'corinthians paulista' → ('corinthians','SP'); 'flamengo do piaui' → ('flamengo','PI'); 'guarani de juazeiro' → ('guarani','CE'); 'vitoria f c' → ('vitoria','ES'); 'atletico cearense' → ('atletico cearense','CE')? not needed (dominan