21,10 @@\n     \"guarani de juazeiro\": (\"guarani\", \"CE\"),\n     \"vitoria f c\": (\"vitoria\", \"ES\"),\n     \"vitoria es\": (\"vitoria\", \"ES\"),\n     \"club america\": (\"club america\", \"\"),\n-    \"boavista\": (\"boavista fc\", \"\"),\n+    \"boavista sport\": (\"boavista\", \"RJ\"),\n+    \"boavista sc saquarema\": (\"boavista\", \"RJ\"),\n }\n \n PREFERRED_DISPLAYS: dict[str, str] = {\n     \"ceara-ce\": \"Ceará\",\n","additions":2,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"src/brasil_mcp/normalize.py","time":{"start":1788228708766,"end":1788228708770}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand:\n- Cup file has 'Boavista - RJ' (15×) → parse: full_key 'boavista rj' → not in aliases → tokens [boavista, rj] → uf RJ → [boavista] → alias check 'boavista' → HIT! → returns ('boavista fc', 'RJ', None) → 'boavista fc-rj'. That's where 'boavista fc-rj' came from — the alias applies AFTER uf extraction too (my code checks alias on the post-uf-stripped tokens). So bare 'boavista' alias maps the base to 'boavista fc' and keeps the RJ.\n\nSo alias ('boavista fc', '') created 'boavista fc-rj' — worse. I need the alias to map 'Boavista - RJ' (the Brazilian cup club) to the Brazilian canonical 'boavista-rj', while bare FIFA 'Boavista' (no uf) maps to the Portuguese club 'boavista fc'. \n\nDesign fix: alias entries should be base-level, and the canonical should use the EXTRACTED uf when present. My current code: `base, alias_uf = alias; return base, uf or alias_uf, country` — for 'Boavista - RJ': base 'boavista fc', uf 'RJ' → 'boavista fc-rj' ✗. What I want: base 'boavista' with uf RJ → 'boavista-rj' ✓, and bare 'Boavista' → 'boavista fc' (no uf).\n\nThe problem is the alias serves two masters. Split into two alias tables:\n- FULL_NAME_ALIASES: raw-string → (base, uf) — for official full names where the whole name maps to a specific club: 'vasco da gama' → ('vasco','RJ'), 'atletico mineiro' → ('atletico','MG'), 'sport club do recife' → ('sport','PE'), 'club america' → ('club america', None), 'boavista' → hmm — 'boavista' bare is ambiguous...\n\nActually the semantics I want for 'boavista': with uf RJ → Brazilian boavista-rj; without uf → Portuguese. So make it a BASE_ALIAS: 'boavista' → base stays 'boavista'... that's identity, no alias needed! The real issue is only FIFA club 'Boavista' (no uf) resolving to 'boavista-rj' via base dominance ({RJ}). \n\nNew approach — FIFA-specific handling in the STORE rather than in normalize: when observing FIFA clubs / resolving FIFA clubs for squads, treat certain names as foreign. Honestly, simplest robust solution: an explicit FOREIGN_FIFA_CLUBS set = {'Boavista'} — in load_all, when observing FIFA clubs, skip names in that set (or observe them under a 'foreign:' prefixed canonical). Then:\n- 'Boavista - RJ' → boavista-rj (Brazilian) ✓\n- FIFA 'Boavista' never observed → resolve('Boavista') → parse → base 'boavista' (no alias now) → _resolve_uf → RJ (from cup data) → 'boavista-rj' → squad_of('Boavista') returns the 27 Portuguese players labeled Boavista ✗ wrong-ish...\n\nHmm. The FIFA club Boavista genuinely is Boavista FC (Portugal). Players: 27 Portuguese-league players. If someone queries \"players at Boavista\" they'd get those 27 — which per the FIFA dataset is CORRECT (it's the Portuguese club). But brazilian_clubs_with_squads must NOT list Boavista as Brazilian.\n\nCleanest: in store._players_by_club building and brazilian_clubs_with_squads / squad_of, exclude foreign FIFA clubs by an explicit set. And remove the 'boavista' alias (it broke 'Boavista - RJ').\n\nImplementation:\n- normalize.py: remove \"boavista\" alias. Add \"boavista sport club\" alias? parse('Boavista Sport Club (antigo...) - RJ') → uf RJ → tokens [boavista, sport, club] → drop 'club' → [boavista, sport] → base 'boavista sport' → 'boavista sport-rj' — but 'Boavista - RJ' → base 'boavista' → 'boavista-rj'. TWO canonicals for the same Brazilian club ✗. Fix: alias 'boavista sport' → ('boavista', 'RJ')... wait alias checked on post-uf tokens: tokens [boavista, sport] joined 'boavista sport' → alias ('boavista','RJ') → base 'boavista', uf already RJ → 'boavista-rj' ✓ merged!\n- store.py: FOREIGN_FIFA_CLUBS = {'Boavista'}; when building _players_by_club: `if player.club in FOREIGN_FIFA_CLUBS: continue`. That kills the cross-file link for Boavista entirely. brazilian_clubs_with_squads then excludes it ✓. player_search(club='Boavista') → club_ids={boavista-rj} → no players → helpful note ✓ fine.\n\nAlso there's 'Boavista SC Saquarema' (brf, 5×) → parse: tokens [boavista, sc, saquarema] → no uf... 'SC Saquarema' — filter 'sc' → [boavista, saquarema] → base 'boavista saquarema' — another split! Alias 'boavista saquarema' → ('boavista','RJ')? Hmm 'SC' here is part of the club name (Sport Club). base 'boavista saquarema' — add alias mapping to boavista-rj. Wait, or generic: alias 'boavista sc saquarema' won't hit post-filter... The alias check happens twice: on post-uf tokens (pre-legal-filter): [boavista, sc, saquarema] → 'boavista sc saquarema' → alias hit if I add it. Add \"boavista sc saquarema\": (\"boavista\", \"RJ\"). ✓ And 'Boavista RJ' (brf) → uf RJ → [boavista] → 'boavista-rj' ✓. 'Boavista' (brf, 3×) → no uf → base 'boavista' → _resolve_uf → RJ (dominant) → boavista-rj ✓ (brf's bare Boavista IS the Brazilian club — good, base dominance works here since FIFA club excluded from... wait, FIFA club IS observed into registry via load_all → _base_ufs['boavista'][''] += 27 → doesn't add uf → distinct still {RJ} ✓ resolves boavista-rj ✓. But display: unsuffixed candidates include FIFA 'Boavista' (27) vs brf 'Boavista' (3) — same string 'Boavista' → count 30 → display 'Boavista' for boavista-rj ✓ fine.)\n\nSo FIFA club observation stays (harmless for resolution), but the STORE excludes it from club linkage via FOREIGN_FIFA_CLUBS.\n\nAny other foreign FIFA clubs colliding with Brazilian bases? From the FIFA Brazilian-ish club scan earlier: América de Cali (base 'america de cali' distinct ✓), Club América (aliased ✓), Athletic Club de Bilbao → [de, bilbao]?? wait 'Athletic Club de Bilbao' → tokens [athletic, club, de, bilbao] → drop legal {athletic, club} → [de, bilbao] → base 'de bilbao' — ugly but distinct ✓ no collision. Charlton Athletic → [charlton] ✓. Oldham Athletic ✓. St. Patrick's Athletic → 'st patricks'? tokens [st, patricks, athletic] → 'st' in LEGAL_WORDS → [patricks] ✓. Vitória Guimarães / Setúbal ✓ distinct. Atlético Nacional/Madrid/etc ✓ distinct. Santos Laguna ✓. Botafogo — FIFA has only Brazilian Botafogo ✓. \n\nSo only Boavista needs the exclusion. \n\nNow the OTHER bug from that output: 'Botafogo-RJ' displayed fine; queries syntax error fixed. Let me apply the Boavista fixes.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05abca98f0013yevuIWkc9rBm6","sessionID":"ses_fa566b1a7ffetmaN0ZgRQlI0ol","messageID":"msg_05abbc512001lop4vPcd0oiNIp"}}
