        print(\"STILL LISTED:\", c)\n# which players link to boavista-rj?\nfrom brasil_mcp.normalize import parse_team_name\nprint(parse_team_name(\"Boavista\"))\nprint(\"resolve:\", store.resolve_team(\"Boavista\"))\nplayers = store._players_by_club.get(\"boavista-rj\", [])\nprint(\"players linked to boavista-rj:\", len(players), {p.club for p in players})\nEOF","time":{"start":1788229349075,"end":1788229349092}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Multiple discoveries:\n\n1. **Venue filters**: Flamengo 2019 all-comps: home 28, away 27. With Série A filter: 19/19 ✓. Fix tests to use competition filter.\n\n2. **2021 CdB \"finals\" is WRONG in my implementation**: For 2021, the max round in the CUP FILE... the query returned round-of-16-ish matches (ABC x Flamengo etc. in early August 2021 — those look like THIRD round). The 2021 CdB final (Atlético-MG x Athletico-PR, Nov/Dec 2021) is missing! Why? Because the cup FILE only covers 2012-2021 seasons — wait it says seasons 2012-2021, and 2021 is included. The 2021 final in the cup file: rounds — max round for 2021... The query for season 2021 stage 'final' returned matches from round... these look like round 5-6. So `self._cup_final_rounds[2021]` = the round number of these matches — meaning the cup file's 2021 season DOESN'T include the final! The 2021 CdB final was played... hold on, actually: the 2021 Copa do Brasil final between Atlético-MG and Athletico-PR was in December 2021 (two legs Dec 15 & 19?). Hmm wait no — actually I need to check what rounds exist in the cup file for 2021. The dataset might end mid-tournament for 2021 (data scraped before the final). Let me check the cup file 2021 max date and rounds.\n\n   Actually looking at the output: 2021 \"finals\" = matches on 2021-07-27..08-06 — that's the round-of-16 (round 5?). If the cup file's 2021 coverage ends there (scraped August 2021), then the \"final\" per my max-round rule = round 5 (which is quarterfinals?). The data just doesn't have the actual 2021 final. So my stage='final' logic returns the last round present — misleading but data-driven. For the TEST, assert the 2020 final (Grêmio x Palmeiras, which IS in the data) and don't test 2021 finals.\n\n   Hmm, but there's a subtlety: for stage='final' on Copa do Brasil I should ideally only return matches when the final round IS the actual final... unknowable from data. Keep max-round rule; test with 2020 and 2012.\n\n   Wait, actually let me check the cup file: does it have round 7/8 matches in 2021? The rounds counter earlier: round 8 x14, round 7 x30. If 2021 had round 7 (semis) + 8 (final)... The 2021 final legs would be dated Nov-Dec 2021. The query returned Aug matches as \"final\" — so 2021's max round in cup file must be 5 or 6. OK.\n\n3. **2019 top scorer**: Flamengo 86 goals (best_records filters teams with ≥30 matches — all Série A teams qualify; but wait earlier direct standings said Flamengo goals_for=94?! Now best_records says 86?? Discrepancy! standings(2019) Flamengo GF=94 (real: 94? actual 2019 Flamengo scored 86 in the league... hmm real history: Flamengo 2019: 86 goals for? Let me think: Flamengo 2019 Série A: 90 pts, 86 GF, 39 GA? Hmm no — earlier standings output showed 'goals_for': 94? Let me recheck: the earlier standings output for 2019 champion: {'team': 'Flamengo', 'matches': 40, ... 'goals_for': 94, 'goals_against': 42...} — that was BEFORE the final dedupe fixes (matches 40 = double-counted). After fixes, standings champion showed 90 pts/38 matches (didn't print GF). Now best_records says 86. Real Flamengo 2019: 86 goals ✓ (they scored 86, conceded 37? something like that). best_records computes from all 380 matches ✓ consistent. My test asserted goals_for == 94 (from the earlier buggy run) — wrong. Fix test to 86. Wait — but where did 94 come from earlier? Earlier run pre-dedupe-fix had 40 matches with duplicated goals. 86 is correct per current data. Also actual real-world: Flamengo 2019 scored 86 ✓✓. And standings test asserts champion points 90 ✓ but nothing about GF. OK.\n\n4. **Boavista STILL in squads list!** My FOREIGN_FIFA_CLUBS edit... let me check store.py is correct. Maybe another FIFA club name maps to boavista-rj — e.g. FIFA club 'Boavista FC'? Or 'Boavista' exists with different casing? Let me check the FIFA club names containing 'boavista'.\n\n5. **Palmeiras 2013**: Série A 38? NO WAIT — the output shows Flamengo 38 every season ✓ (I switched to Flamengo). Palmeiras 2013 was Série B — the TEST used Palmeiras; fix to Flamengo.\n\n6. **seasons coverage test**: rows[SERIE_A]['seasons'] == '2003-2023' ✓ passes now; earlier failure was because... the failure output earlier said test_season_coverage FAILED. With current data it should pass. Maybe earlier failure was rows[COPA_DO_BRASIL]['matches'] > 2000? It's 2093 ✓. Lib seasons '2013-2022' ✓. Hmm — earlier failure may have been the same stale-session effect... no wait, tests run fresh. Let me re-run and see which fail now.\n\n7. **2015 phantom persists** (381). The Brasilia FC match: it's season-labeled 2015 via my month≤2 rule (2016-01-30 → 2015) but it's not a 2015 Série A fixture. My standings filter (≥50% matches) keeps standings clean, but the raw count is 381. For the dedupe test, adjust to allow {380, 381} or better: exclude it? Hmm — what IS this match? 'Brasilia FC 1-1 CA Taguatinga' — actually this is a CANDANGÃO (Brasília state league) match mislabeled 'Serie A' in brf. I can't detect that generically... Options: (a) test tolerance for 2015; (b) drop brf 'Serie A/B/C' matches whose date is in Jan/Feb AND (both teams) have no other matches that season in that competition... (b) is principled: the phantom's teams (Brasilia FC, CA Taguatinga) play exactly 1 Série A match in 2015 — no real Série A team plays only 1 match. But dropping data risks losing legit postponed-tail matches (e.g., a Série C match delayed to Feb whose teams played a full season). Actually a cleaner data-driven rule for LOAD-time season assignment: for brf Jan/Feb matches, reassigning to season-1 only makes sense if BOTH teams have a plausible presence in that season-1 competition. Even simpler: when reassigning brf Jan/Feb matches to year-1, check that both teams have ≥3 matches in (competition, year-1) from OTHER sources; else keep original year (or mark as-is). Hmm — for the 2020 COVID tail: Vasco x Goiás Feb 2021 → season 2020 — do both teams have ≥3 matches in 2020 Série A from other sources? YES (bras covers 2020 fully). For the Brasilia phantom: Brasilia FC in 2015 Série A from other sources? NO → keep season 2016... then it pollutes 2016 (381 for 2016!). Ugh. It pollutes SOME season either way because it's mislabeled tournament-wise, not season-wise.\n\n   Alternative: drop it? It's 1 match of pure noise in a dataset with a known error. A principled drop rule: brf 'Serie A' matches in Jan/Feb where NEITHER team appears in that competition's season from authoritative sources → exclude from Série A? That's getting too specific. Pragmatic: leave it; test tolerance: seasons 2012-2022 have {380, 381} matches (2015 = 381 with one known mislabeled fixture from the source data). I'll note it in README as a known data quirk. Actually — hmm, let me reconsider. The standings filter already neutralizes its effect on tables. The count test just needs the tolerance. Fine.\n\n8. **suggest('Palmeirass')** — improve suggest with difflib.\n\nLet me check Boavista first, then fix all tests + improve suggest.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ac667ba0014RnrLNgdw3Pq3I","sessionID":"ses_fa566b1a7ffetmaN0ZgRQlI0ol","messageID":"msg_05ac599a700103XMsXJCzKvDBO"}}
